412
00:19:58.350 --> 00:19:59.950
But, yeah, what a great, funny way to do it.

413
00:20:00.050 --> 00:20:00.270
Yeah.

414
00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:01.250
For, like, three weeks.

415
00:20:01.670 --> 00:20:05.270
Do you have any, like, offset behind-the-scenes memories?

416
00:20:05.410 --> 00:20:09.030
Like, when you look back at that time on The Office, what do you think of?

417
00:20:09.430 --> 00:20:10.270
I mean, it was, I don't know.

418
00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:11.050
It was an interesting time.

419
00:20:11.190 --> 00:20:13.950
Like, I mean, I'm trying to think of stuff that would not be interesting to talk about.

420
00:20:14.070 --> 00:20:18.750
Because, I mean, for me, it's like, I think about, you know, like, it felt like kind of a transitional moment in my life.

421
00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:19.630
Like, I was moving.

422
00:20:19.810 --> 00:20:24.510
Like, I literally just moved from, so where they tape here, the podcast, I used to live across the street in this building.

423
00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:28.050
And I had moved out of there and was, like, living in this, like, this house I rented.

424
00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:30.370
Yeah, we should share with everyone.

425
00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:32.570
When you walked in, you were like, oh, my gosh.

426
00:20:32.750 --> 00:20:37.010
From the windows where we record, he can literally see the window of his old apartment.

427
00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:39.030
Yeah, I can see my old apartment across the street here.

428
00:20:39.930 --> 00:20:40.630
Yes, I don't know.

429
00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:44.830
So, for me, it was an interesting, it was such a fun, weird kind of year.

430
00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:52.650
Because it was like, you know, just being on the show felt like this fun gift, like, that was just put in my lap that I got to do for a year.

431
00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:54.250
So, it was just kind of pure joy.

432
00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:59.030
Because, I mean, you know, like I said earlier, a lot of shows are not that fun to shoot.

433
00:20:59.170 --> 00:21:04.350
Like, I mean, especially now that everything is kind of just like, a lot of TV shows now are like movies that never end.

434
00:21:04.470 --> 00:21:05.530
Like, single camera stuff.

435
00:21:05.530 --> 00:21:09.750
It's like, you know, you can go shoot a movie for, like, two or three months and do, like, the crazy hours.

436
00:21:09.750 --> 00:21:11.870
Because it's only for, like, you know, a couple of months.

437
00:21:12.270 --> 00:21:15.470
But doing that, like, nine months a year, like, really, like, wears on you after a while.

438
00:21:15.650 --> 00:21:15.870
Yeah.

439
00:21:17.650 --> 00:21:20.230
So, yeah, it was such a fun year.

440
00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:27.030
You know, the main thing that kind of took me aback, because I'd never done a show that did that many episodes a year.

441
00:21:27.670 --> 00:21:28.030
Right.

442
00:21:28.030 --> 00:21:36.390
So, it was really interesting for me just to watch the kind of, like, ebbs and flows of, like, everybody's got so much energy, you know, at firSt.

443
00:21:36.630 --> 00:21:40.390
And then by, like, like, I remember there's, like, a hiatus, like, like, midway through.

444
00:21:40.530 --> 00:21:43.350
And it's like, you need it because everyone's about to die.

445
00:21:43.490 --> 00:21:43.790
Right.

446
00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:44.950
You know, from, like, exhaustion.

447
00:21:45.350 --> 00:21:51.810
And then it was, like, the scripts after that, like, and everybody's energy, like, okay, everybody's got way more energy again, you know, to kind of, like, carry you through.

448
00:21:51.810 --> 00:22:02.230
It was interesting for me just to watch the sort of endurance and the kind of rhythm of the thing and how you had to kind of pace yourself a little bit.

449
00:22:02.310 --> 00:22:04.330
And also just how fast stuff would come out.

450
00:22:04.410 --> 00:22:08.730
Like, we would shoot an episode and it'd be on TV, like, was it, like, two or three weeks later?

451
00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:09.770
Like, really faSt.

452
00:22:09.970 --> 00:22:10.270
Yeah.

453
00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:11.670
The turnaround was really quick.

454
00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:13.070
Yeah, the turnaround blew my mind.

455
00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:13.550
Yeah.

456
00:22:13.730 --> 00:22:16.650
Because I'd also never been on anything that turned around that fast before.

457
00:22:17.150 --> 00:22:21.930
Well, we'd get a script and we'd read it midweek and then we'd shoot it the next week.

458
00:22:22.090 --> 00:22:22.210
Yeah.

459
00:22:22.570 --> 00:22:22.910
Yeah.

460
00:22:23.090 --> 00:22:23.330
Yeah.

461
00:22:23.670 --> 00:22:30.230
So, yeah, I think for me just the process of it was really interesting to watch as far as the offsets.

462
00:22:30.330 --> 00:22:31.230
Well, I guess that's still on set.

463
00:22:31.370 --> 00:22:32.830
But that was cool to me.

464
00:22:32.930 --> 00:22:40.390
I mean, one thing that I, you know, liked about the show so much and was so excited about when I initially got the offer was the idea of, you know,

465
00:22:40.450 --> 00:22:45.410
eventually being like BJ or Mindy and being like a writer, producer kind of role on the show too.

466
00:22:45.410 --> 00:22:46.430
Had it went on longer.

467
00:22:46.910 --> 00:22:53.230
I'm so curious because, you know, over in the little accounting nook, we had our different bits that we would do with one another.

468
00:22:53.550 --> 00:22:58.390
Did you and Jake back in the annex, like sitting across from each other, did you have like bits that you did?

469
00:23:00.430 --> 00:23:01.970
Did you have the Zen garden?

470
00:23:02.950 --> 00:23:03.350
No.

