00:40:48.210 --> 00:40:48.890
I don't know.

986
00:40:49.050 --> 00:40:50.210
You know, everything cycles.

987
00:40:50.690 --> 00:40:51.270
It does.

988
00:40:51.270 --> 00:40:51.870
It does.

989
00:40:52.070 --> 00:40:58.510
I was listening to, I listened to this podcast, this film history show called You Must Remember This.

990
00:40:58.610 --> 00:40:59.170
Do you know this show?

991
00:40:59.530 --> 00:40:59.890
No.

992
00:41:00.090 --> 00:41:01.070
No, but I'm writing it down.

993
00:41:01.270 --> 00:41:01.670
It's very good.

994
00:41:02.090 --> 00:41:10.550
You know, it'll just give you, like, a reminder of, like, how, you know, like, the parallels between, like, in the 50s when television came along and the studios were collapsing,

995
00:41:10.550 --> 00:41:13.950
you know, and this, like, there's a lot of parallels between that and now.

996
00:41:14.650 --> 00:41:17.330
And just as a reminder that, like, you know, everything cycles.

997
00:41:18.030 --> 00:41:21.250
Well, what will happen is someone will make a great comedy.

998
00:41:21.370 --> 00:41:21.490
Yep.

999
00:41:21.490 --> 00:41:27.390
It'll be an outlier and it'll hit big and then we'll go into the season of comedy again.

1000
00:41:27.410 --> 00:41:27.530
Yeah.

1001
00:41:27.730 --> 00:41:28.350
I think we're due.

1002
00:41:28.530 --> 00:41:29.210
Or something like that.

1003
00:41:29.370 --> 00:41:29.650
Yeah.

1004
00:41:29.650 --> 00:41:34.390
I think we're due for, like, a whole new group of people, a whole new, like, style of comedy.

1005
00:41:34.390 --> 00:41:39.470
Like, you know, because it kind of felt like, you know, like, I had, like, like, one line in, like, Superbad.

1006
00:41:39.470 --> 00:41:43.290
And it was, like, it feels like everyone in Superbad got a career.

1007
00:41:43.630 --> 00:41:43.910
Yeah.

1008
00:41:43.910 --> 00:41:48.030
It felt like that was, like, a graduating class a little bit, you know, and we're all still kind of still around.

1009
00:41:48.630 --> 00:41:53.070
And I don't know, like, what the equivalent movie to that since then is.

1010
00:41:53.690 --> 00:41:56.550
Like, it felt like there was, like, a scene, like, UCB and, like, I don't know.

1011
00:41:56.670 --> 00:41:56.910
Right, right.

1012
00:41:57.230 --> 00:41:59.130
Because I feel like it's like you said you've been around long enough, you know.

1013
00:41:59.230 --> 00:42:04.990
Like, I feel like I still know and see, like, everybody from, you know, 20 years ago, whatever that was.

1014
00:42:05.190 --> 00:42:07.930
I still, like, hang out with my friends from ImprovOlympic.

1015
00:42:08.090 --> 00:42:08.190
Yeah.

1016
00:42:08.190 --> 00:42:11.810
You know, so it's like that group you kind of come up together with.

1017
00:42:12.410 --> 00:42:16.350
All right, Clark, it is time for our call sheet questions.

1018
00:42:16.790 --> 00:42:16.990
Okay.

1019
00:42:17.310 --> 00:42:21.530
What was your first entertainment job that you were paid for?

1020
00:42:21.930 --> 00:42:28.810
I believe it was a commercial when I was, like, like five or six years old.

1021
00:42:28.910 --> 00:42:29.530
Come on.

1022
00:42:29.830 --> 00:42:36.930
It was either a commercial or it was I did a pilot that Carrie Fisher wrote and produced.

1023
00:42:36.930 --> 00:42:38.530
And Debbie Reynolds starred in.

1024
00:42:38.690 --> 00:42:39.010
What?

1025
00:42:39.230 --> 00:42:40.750
When I was, like, six.

1026
00:42:40.970 --> 00:42:42.190
Were you a child actor?

1027
00:42:42.230 --> 00:42:42.570
Yes.

1028
00:42:42.750 --> 00:42:43.030
What?

1029
00:42:43.250 --> 00:42:43.470
Yes.

1030
00:42:43.470 --> 00:42:44.190
I did not know this.

1031
00:42:44.270 --> 00:42:44.690
Did you know this?

1032
00:42:44.710 --> 00:42:45.490
I did not know this.

1033
00:42:45.510 --> 00:42:45.710
Yes.

1034
00:42:45.850 --> 00:42:46.910
First of all, I need to back it up.

1035
00:42:46.910 --> 00:42:49.390
My SAG card says, like, 1991 on it.

1036
00:42:49.570 --> 00:42:49.990
Okay, wait.

1037
00:42:50.290 --> 00:42:50.510
Yeah.

1038
00:42:50.810 --> 00:42:51.610
That's not a joke.

1039
00:42:52.170 --> 00:42:52.470
Okay.

1040
00:42:52.750 --> 00:42:54.130
Member since 1991.

1041
00:42:54.670 --> 00:42:55.650
I need you to back up.

1042
00:42:55.690 --> 00:42:57.010
That's the year I graduated from high school.

1043
00:42:57.070 --> 00:42:59.230
Well, I was already, I already had a full-time job.

1044
00:42:59.310 --> 00:42:59.670
You did.

1045
00:43:00.030 --> 00:43:00.330
So.

1046
00:43:01.030 --> 00:43:01.990
Wait, wait, wait, wait.

1047
00:43:02.330 --> 00:43:02.730
Wait.

1048
00:43:02.870 --> 00:43:03.910
I need to know.

1049
00:43:04.110 --> 00:43:05.510
I have so many questions now.

1050
00:43:06.470 --> 00:43:08.070
You are living in Arkansas.

1051
00:43:08.410 --> 00:43:08.550
Yeah.

1052
00:43:08.710 --> 00:43:10.310
And you became a child actor in Arkansas.

1053
00:43:10.630 --> 00:43:11.010
No.

1054
00:43:11.290 --> 00:43:11.530
No.

1055
00:43:11.730 --> 00:43:18.650
My mother had a childhood friend living in LA that we came and visited that was working as an actress.

1056
00:43:18.650 --> 00:43:19.170
Okay.

1057
00:43:19.530 --> 00:43:21.670
And her manager saw me.

1058
00:43:22.170 --> 00:43:24.030
And was like, this kid's got to be in showbiz.

1059
00:43:24.050 --> 00:43:25.030
We got to get this kid out.

1060
00:43:25.090 --> 00:43:25.930
We got to send him out.

1061
00:43:27.030 --> 00:43:29.330
And, like, sent me on, I think, a commercial.

1062
00:43:29.490 --> 00:43:32.570
I mean, my mother can answer this better than I could because I was, you know, like five years old.

1063
00:43:32.690 --> 00:43:33.970
But, yeah, booked a bunch of commercials.

1064
00:43:34.430 --> 00:43:35.030
And then.

1065
00:43:35.370 --> 00:43:37.390
Do you remember than any of the commercials?

1066
00:43:37.570 --> 00:43:38.510
Like, what were the products?

1067
00:43:38.770 --> 00:43:38.870
Like.

1068
00:43:38.870 --> 00:43:40.250
Like Toyota Kellogg's.

1069
00:43:40.410 --> 00:43:41.390
There was a Pop-Tarts one that was.

1070
00:43:41.510 --> 00:43:42.930
And they were all, like, big national spots.

1071
00:43:43.270 --> 00:43:43.590
Wow.

1072
00:43:49.230 --> 00:43:51.490
How old were you when you had a holding deal?

1073
00:43:51.690 --> 00:43:52.750
Like six or seven.

1074
00:43:52.950 --> 00:43:53.590
Like something like that.

1075
00:43:54.210 --> 00:43:55.690
How was that for you?

1076
00:43:55.930 --> 00:43:56.210
Did you.

1077
00:43:56.390 --> 00:43:56.730
It was fun.

1078
00:43:56.990 --> 00:43:58.290
Like, there was no pressure.

1079
00:43:58.650 --> 00:43:58.850
You didn't.

1080
00:43:59.010 --> 00:43:59.290
There was no pressure.

1081
00:43:59.410 --> 00:43:59.870
You're a child.

1082
00:44:00.010 --> 00:44:00.130
You know.

1083
00:44:00.190 --> 00:44:00.450
Yeah.

1084
00:44:00.630 --> 00:44:00.790
Yeah.

1085
00:44:00.970 --> 00:44:04.730
Because I was a very, like, gregarious, you know, like, talkative.

1086
00:44:05.910 --> 00:44:08.870
But would you have to leave school to do these projects?

1087
00:44:08.950 --> 00:44:10.530
And how was that socially?

1088
00:44:10.810 --> 00:44:14.090
You were, like, the guy who was going to LA from Arkansas?

1089
00:44:14.090 --> 00:44:16.090
Yeah, but, I mean, you're, like, such a little kid.

1090
00:44:16.090 --> 00:44:19.430
I feel like it's before you're, like, any, like.

1091
00:44:19.430 --> 00:44:19.650
That's true.

1092
00:44:19.750 --> 00:44:21.430
It's before anyone's really aware of it.

1093
00:44:21.430 --> 00:44:22.810
It's before neurotic stuff sets in.

1094
00:44:22.930 --> 00:44:23.110
Yeah.

1095
00:44:23.270 --> 00:44:23.450
You know.

1096
00:44:23.610 --> 00:44:24.790
They're just, like, Clark's back.

1097
00:44:24.950 --> 00:44:25.230
Okay.

1098
00:44:25.330 --> 00:44:25.830
Hi, Clark.

1099
00:44:26.110 --> 00:44:26.630
And there were.

1100
00:44:26.710 --> 00:44:30.930
And then I ended up on this sitcom called Hearts of Fire.

1101
00:44:31.230 --> 00:44:34.950
This are John Ritter and Marky Post and Billy Bob Thornton and Ed Asner.

1102
00:44:35.730 --> 00:44:36.450
Crazy caSt.

1103
00:44:36.770 --> 00:44:39.590
And Billy Bob's also from Arkansas, as most people probably know.

1104
00:44:40.070 --> 00:44:41.530
Like, the same place that I'm from.

1105
00:44:41.530 --> 00:44:45.030
And did that for, like, three years.

1106
00:44:45.130 --> 00:44:48.690
And then after that, went back to school in Arkansas.

1107
00:44:48.690 --> 00:44:53.870
Because my mom, you know, wanted me to have a normal, like, school and childhood experience.

1108
00:44:53.910 --> 00:44:58.450
And it was, like, she was insistent that I, like, go to school and go to college and all that stuff.

1109
00:44:58.490 --> 00:45:02.590
So then, yeah, went through high school in Arkansas and then came back out here for college.

1110
00:45:02.790 --> 00:45:03.350
Okay, wait.

1111
00:45:03.350 --> 00:45:04.230
And then started working again.

1112
00:45:04.370 --> 00:45:04.890
I have a question.

1113
00:45:05.010 --> 00:45:08.610
So when you go back to school in Arkansas, do you still have that acting bug?

1114
00:45:08.610 --> 00:45:10.690
Like, are you doing theater in high school?

1115
00:45:10.930 --> 00:45:11.870
Are you doing the plays?

1116
00:45:12.270 --> 00:45:12.910
I wasn't.

1117
00:45:13.030 --> 00:45:16.290
I did very much have, like, I knew I wanted to come back.

1118
00:45:16.730 --> 00:45:20.330
But I, since I was about 12, I really wanted to be a movie director.

1119
00:45:20.490 --> 00:45:21.670
That was really what I always wanted to do.

1120
00:45:22.050 --> 00:45:24.530
And, yeah, of course that was from, you know, being out here.

1121
00:45:24.650 --> 00:45:29.190
Like, because those were, like, thank God, like, I don't have any, like, child actor horror stories.

1122
00:45:29.290 --> 00:45:30.870
Like, those were, like, really fun years for me.

1123
00:45:30.870 --> 00:45:36.710
Like, when I think back about, like, childhood and stuff, like, I have just, like, really fond, you know, kind of bizarre memories.

1124
00:45:37.110 --> 00:45:37.790
Oh, man.

1125
00:45:38.610 --> 00:45:38.830
Yeah.

1126
00:45:39.110 --> 00:45:40.990
That's so fascinating to me.

1127
00:45:41.170 --> 00:45:44.810
So that was, yeah, that was one of my many lives I've lived.

1128
00:45:45.110 --> 00:45:47.270
I mean, question number one.

1129
00:45:47.430 --> 00:45:47.810
I know.

1130
00:45:48.810 --> 00:45:50.470
Mind some goals there.

1131
00:45:51.430 --> 00:45:52.690
Question number two.

1132
00:45:53.050 --> 00:45:54.970
Do you speak any other languages?

1133
00:45:55.430 --> 00:45:55.770
No.

1134
00:45:56.390 --> 00:45:56.790
No.

1135
00:45:56.990 --> 00:45:57.510
I wish.

1136
00:45:57.790 --> 00:46:00.030
We can move on quickly from question number two.

1137
00:46:00.030 --> 00:46:04.070
You know, I took Spanish in high school and college, but it's like, if you don't use it, you forget it.

1138
00:46:04.170 --> 00:46:05.210
And, yeah, I wish.

1139
00:46:05.450 --> 00:46:05.650
Yeah.

1140
00:46:06.090 --> 00:46:07.570
Well, do you play a musical instrument?

1141
00:46:08.030 --> 00:46:08.830
I can play guitar.

1142
00:46:09.330 --> 00:46:12.610
Not, like, excellently or anything, but, yeah, play guitar.

1143
00:46:12.830 --> 00:46:13.250
All right.

1144
00:46:13.410 --> 00:46:15.390
I had a phase when I lived in the building across the street.

1145
00:46:15.490 --> 00:46:17.430
We DJed for a while, which was fun.

1146
00:46:20.630 --> 00:46:23.550
I guess DJing is a type of instrument, really.

1147
00:46:23.550 --> 00:46:24.170
It really was.

1148
00:46:24.290 --> 00:46:28.590
I mean, it was in the, like, it was in the, like, when there was, like, it was the last time there was kind of a real scene.

1149
00:46:28.590 --> 00:46:30.430
It was right before, like, camera phones, basically.

1150
00:46:30.750 --> 00:46:32.030
Did you have a DJ name?

1151
00:46:32.430 --> 00:46:32.710
Yeah.

1152
00:46:32.850 --> 00:46:37.650
Actually, me and my friend Chris Holmes, who's still a huge DJ, he's Paul McCartney's opening act.

1153
00:46:37.810 --> 00:46:38.810
Oh, my gosh.

1154
00:46:38.930 --> 00:46:40.890
But we called ourselves Show Business.

1155
00:46:42.310 --> 00:46:43.430
Which I thought was pretty good.

1156
00:46:43.530 --> 00:46:44.910
Business with an S or a Z?

1157
00:46:45.270 --> 00:46:45.810
With an S.

1158
00:46:45.950 --> 00:46:46.430
With an S.

1159
00:46:47.610 --> 00:46:49.470
Show Business taking the stage.

1160
00:46:49.530 --> 00:46:52.850
We had, Chris made business cards one time, and they said Show Business.

1161
00:46:52.850 --> 00:46:56.130
And then on the back, I just said No Request, which I thought was funny.

1162
00:46:56.770 --> 00:46:58.330
With no contact info, either.

1163
00:46:59.170 --> 00:46:59.890
Just that.

1164
00:47:00.150 --> 00:47:00.530
Just that.

1165
00:47:01.030 --> 00:47:04.450
What's a place that you've been to that you absolutely loved?

1166
00:47:04.770 --> 00:47:06.430
I mean, I've been to a lot of places I liked.

1167
00:47:07.450 --> 00:47:17.230
I really enjoyed getting to film in London, just because the, and getting to, like, live there for, like, an extended period, because I did all the touristy stuff, like,

1168
00:47:17.250 --> 00:47:21.570
the walking tours and all that, you know, like, the Jack the Ripper historical walking tour.

1169
00:47:21.670 --> 00:47:22.690
Like, I did all, like, with the group.

1170
00:47:22.870 --> 00:47:25.310
It's, like, me and the other group walking around with a little map.

1171
00:47:25.470 --> 00:47:31.490
So that was pretty fun, because, I mean, it's one thing, you know, to, like, visit somewhere, but to get to, like, kind of, like, live somewhere for a while.

1172
00:47:31.490 --> 00:47:32.750
You know what was bizarre about that?

1173
00:47:32.890 --> 00:47:40.950
When I left that season, The Office 2, Kick-Ass 2, that was in London, and I was living at these furnished apartments like the Oakwood.

1174
00:47:41.130 --> 00:47:42.370
I don't know if people know what the Oakwood is.

1175
00:47:42.450 --> 00:47:43.790
Well, it's not even the Oakwood anymore, but you know what I mean.

1176
00:47:43.790 --> 00:47:47.690
Yeah, it's just furnished apartments that a lot of people stay in when they're filming.

1177
00:47:48.030 --> 00:47:48.650
Corporate housing.

1178
00:47:48.910 --> 00:47:54.110
And the only other person that I ever saw every day in, like, the gym or the lobby was John Malkovich.

1179
00:47:54.810 --> 00:47:58.930
And we never spoke, and I didn't, like, I mean, I think I said, like, hello to him.

1180
00:47:58.930 --> 00:48:03.650
I'm pretty sure I told him this, because that was, like, years before I worked with him in Arkansas.

1181
00:48:04.170 --> 00:48:08.150
But, yeah, getting to, like, spend a lot of time in London was really fun.

1182
00:48:08.510 --> 00:48:16.250
Because I hate long flights, so I don't know if I'll, like, I'm not somebody that, like, travels for fun, because a, like, 12-hour flight is my nightmare.

1183
00:48:16.730 --> 00:48:19.650
And I feel like I spent so many years, like, traveling for work a lot.

1184
00:48:19.810 --> 00:48:20.110
Yeah.

1185
00:48:20.390 --> 00:48:22.010
Which was great, because you get to see a lot of places.

1186
00:48:22.190 --> 00:48:25.570
You know, you get to work in, like, Miami and Atlanta and Vancouver and Toronto and London.

1187
00:48:25.570 --> 00:48:32.810
And I don't know when the next time I'll get back over there is, so I'm happy that I got to, that someone paid me to, you know,

1188
00:48:32.950 --> 00:48:35.410
go on an extended vacation over there was pretty good.

1189
00:48:35.590 --> 00:48:36.950
It's such a special place.

1190
00:48:37.130 --> 00:48:38.750
Jenna lived there, too, for a job.

1191
00:48:38.910 --> 00:48:39.410
I did.

1192
00:48:39.550 --> 00:48:43.110
And it's one of my favorite memories, too, because it's like you said, I really.

1193
00:48:43.330 --> 00:48:44.450
The city's, like, 2,000 years old.

1194
00:48:44.610 --> 00:48:45.530
It's just cool.

1195
00:48:45.590 --> 00:48:46.450
I mean, amazing.

1196
00:48:46.450 --> 00:48:57.230
And also, there's little things about, like, I do love to travel, but I like to stay places as long as possible, so I can do things like, what's their grocery store like?

1197
00:48:57.390 --> 00:48:58.250
No, totally.

1198
00:48:58.370 --> 00:48:58.770
I agree.

1199
00:48:58.910 --> 00:49:01.010
You know, like, how do they sell their fruit here?

1200
00:49:01.250 --> 00:49:01.550
Yeah.

1201
00:49:01.650 --> 00:49:01.910
Right.

1202
00:49:01.910 --> 00:49:04.690
I love details like that, the day-to-day.

1203
00:49:05.030 --> 00:49:07.370
Jenna left me a message one time while she was over there.

1204
00:49:07.410 --> 00:49:11.610
We would leave each other messages, and she was like, well, today I went to buy a microwave.

1205
00:49:12.650 --> 00:49:13.090
Yeah.

1206
00:49:13.090 --> 00:49:15.570
And you had it, like, it was in a book, right?

1207
00:49:15.570 --> 00:49:16.870
Well, it was very strange.

1208
00:49:16.870 --> 00:49:26.610
I had to purchase a microwave for the flat that I was renting, and the store, you walked in, and there was just a guy behind the counter, and then there were, like, three catalogs.

1209
00:49:26.970 --> 00:49:28.450
There weren't items out.

1210
00:49:28.450 --> 00:49:29.010
No iteMs.

1211
00:49:29.090 --> 00:49:39.150
And then you would point to it, and he would go, like, deep in the back bowels of beyond this door behind the counter, and then he would come back with the microwave.

1212
00:49:39.310 --> 00:49:39.950
It was like.

1213
00:49:40.070 --> 00:49:41.670
It's like when you order food and they have the picture?

1214
00:49:42.170 --> 00:49:42.530
Yes.

1215
00:49:42.630 --> 00:49:42.870
Yes.

1216
00:49:43.350 --> 00:49:46.150
Or if, like, Amazon was a store.

1217
00:49:46.390 --> 00:49:46.990
It was just a dude.

1218
00:49:47.110 --> 00:49:50.050
And you just, like, pointed, and then they brought it out to you.

1219
00:49:50.270 --> 00:49:52.090
It was so interesting.

1220
00:49:52.990 --> 00:49:56.730
And that was when I asked the production, where does one go to buy?

1221
00:49:56.730 --> 00:49:58.350
How do you buy a microwave here?

1222
00:49:58.570 --> 00:50:00.050
They're like, oh, you go to this.

1223
00:50:00.710 --> 00:50:01.410
Catalog man.

1224
00:50:01.650 --> 00:50:02.710
Yeah, catalog man.

1225
00:50:02.850 --> 00:50:04.290
She left me that message years ago.

1226
00:50:04.390 --> 00:50:05.710
I think about it all the time.

1227
00:50:06.030 --> 00:50:08.870
